@meta
id: 02-06
title: What counts as one undo
chapter: 02
type: lesson
skills: undo
par: 104
minutes: 5
vscode: Ctrl+Z, which walks back a word or so at a time
open: standup.md

@brief
This is the one that catches everybody coming from VS Code. There,
Ctrl+Z walks backwards through your typing in small bites — a word, a
line, whatever the editor decided was a chunk. Press it five times and
you go back five little steps.

Vim draws the line somewhere else. One trip into insert mode is one
change. Everything you type between pressing `i` or `a` and pressing
`Esc` — one character or four paragraphs — is undone by a single `u`.

Prove it to yourself. In order:

  1. On the Today line, append ` and the 2.14 release notes`. `Esc`.

  2. On the Blocked line, append ` really, unless the payments review
     drags on again`. `Esc`.

  3. Press `u` exactly once.

The Blocked line must come back to `Blocked: nothing` — all of it, in one
key. The Today line must keep its addition. Then save.

If you find yourself pressing `u` a second time, you have already lost:
that would take the Today line with it.

@keys
| `u` | undo one change — one whole insert session counts as one |
| `Esc` | ends the change, which is what makes it undoable as a unit |

@hints
Two separate trips into insert mode means two changes on the undo stack.
One `u` removes the most recent one.
--
Pressing `Esc` is what closes a change. If you had typed both additions
without leaving insert mode in between, they would be one change and one
`u` would remove both.
--
Sequence: `jjj` `$a` text `Esc`, then `j` `$a` text `Esc`, then `u`, then
`ZZ`.

@solution
    jjj $ a  and the 2.14 release notes <Esc>
    j $ a  really, unless the payments review drags on again <Esc>
    u
    ZZ

Once you know where the boundary is, you can put it where you want it.
Press `Esc` between two edits and you can undo them separately; stay in
insert mode and they undo together. Typing a long block of new code in
one sitting means `u` wipes all of it, which is usually what you want
when you decide the block was wrong.

The flip side is the failure people hit in week one: they hold `i`, edit
for ten minutes using arrow keys, then press `u` and watch ten minutes
vanish. That is not vim being hostile. It is vim taking you at your word
that the ten minutes were one change. Short trips into insert mode, `Esc`
often, and the undo history stays shaped like your thinking.

@start standup.md
# Standup, Thursday

Yesterday: finished the refund window ticket
Today: code review
Blocked: nothing

@expected standup.md
# Standup, Thursday

Yesterday: finished the refund window ticket
Today: code review and the 2.14 release notes
Blocked: nothing
